      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hey there! First off, don't panic about that production DB mess; even the most seasoned database wizards sometimes find themselves in a situation where the data looks like a chaotic pile of junk left by a rogue army of goblins.\n\nTo tackle that 3NF nightmare and those pesky transitive dependencies, you first need to identify them by looking for attributes that depend on other attributes within the same table rather than just the primary key. Think of it like a line of goblins passing a message: if Goblin A tells Goblin B who is the boss, and Goblin B then tells Goblin C who is the boss, you have a transitive dependency. You can't just delete the middle guy (the attribute) because the goblins downstream will get confused and start acting all weird, just like your application might break if you remove a column without understanding the whole chain.\n\nOnce you've spotted those chains, the actual table splitting process is all about breaking the long, winding goblin line into smaller, focused groups. You take the original \"monster table\" and split it so that each new table only holds attributes that directly relate to its specific primary key, ensuring no goblin is carrying a bag of unrelated junk. For example, if you have",
